@LZZZZ Hello more likely you need first of all check version of your DE, PipeWire and Alsa UCM configs, if everything pretty new more likely you need to play with Alsa UCM configs to properly match jacks

@nfp0 Hello, I tried surround 5.1 and 7.1 and everything works but except some channels interference with each other if no audio on this channel or it is but very quiet (in general the same with headphone out and rear out in stereo mode - maybe hardware issue), and exactly the same bug I got from Windows VM with connected ALC4080 directly into VM by USB and with Realteck driver installed from site of my mobo - it seems that most things works due it USB UAC 2 compatible,  but ALC4080 just buggy itself, I even think that here no hardware amplifier for microphone and on Windows it works just because software amp inside Realteck driver that act to windows like it has amp...

I just bought external sound card and usb mic and everything works just perfect I even disabled ALC4080 from bios to speedup booting of my OS

Also I have recommendation to everyone that has this codec - disable auto suspend of jack ports and S/PDIF ports on PipeWire or Wireplumber configs, it will avoid issues when audio just disappear after you stopped audio for few seconds - looks like it buggy suspend mode and sometimes it just does not return from suspend mode

@fxzzi Also I would recommend to add something like that for all outputs for your codec - for all analog outputs it reduce or remove different pops after port was suspended that also lead to immediate response instead of just skipping some part of audio (for example message in slack will be immediate instead of hearing just second part of it's sound and without any pops) - I noticed that it very usefull for most USB DACs.
Also I suggest use it for S/PDIF - by some reason it almost remove all problems for me related with this port on this DAC

In general next config just disallow to suspend port after it was used (before first audio it can pop single time but no more during session)

`/usr/share/wireplumber/wireplumber.conf.d/51-disable-suspension.conf`

```
monitor.alsa.rules = [
    {
        matches = [
            {
                node.name = "alsa_output.usb-FIIO_FiiO_K11-01.analog-stereo"
            }
            {
                node.name = "alsa_output.usb-FIIO_FIIO_KA11-01.analog-stereo"
            }
        ]
        actions = {
            update-props = {
                session.suspend-timeout-seconds = 0
            }
        }
    }
]
```

PS: Put here your matches instead of mine - here example only

Firmware most likely does not fix linux issues (at least not all of them)
1. By some reason ALC4080 does not expose all required functionality through USB UAC API - it mean even if firmware will fix microphone volume let say - it will be fixed on windows only due its driver do something additionally directly to ALC4080 to configure codec
2. Codec seems itself buggy (or maybe its firmware) - for example initialization time can be like 30-60seconds - and sometimes it slow down even boot time to DE due DE waiting for some services that waiting to start sound service that waiting for ALC4080
3. And even more - some motherboard has additional AMP for headphones and driver technically can control them manually directly through setting some bits/messages

PS: Just buy any decent external DAC and disable ALC40* in BIOS - you will get way more then playing around with this codec, for example you will get zero issues when GPU fully loaded and it create EMI that interference with mobos codec and create small noises in headphones - with external DAC you eliminate it due device works outside of box

For example just proper DACs that just works and does not bother me for almost 2 years 
Fiio K11 (not R2R version but regular) - desktop version with full bunch of outputs and configurations
Fiio KA11 - small Type-C DAC for smartphones but it has cool DAC inside and proper AMP and it can be configured with smartphone and when you plug into PC it will use those settings automatically 
In both you need to make something like this to avoid it sleep to remove pops from audio system and use non spdif output in sound panel (due it does not control volume)
```
/usr/share/wireplumber/wireplumber.conf.d/51-disable-suspension.conf 
monitor.alsa.rules = [
    {
        matches = [
            {
                node.name = "alsa_output.usb-FIIO_FiiO_K11-01.analog-stereo"
            }
            {
                node.name = "alsa_output.usb-FIIO_FIIO_KA11-01.analog-stereo"
            }
        ]
        actions = {
            update-props = {
                session.suspend-timeout-seconds = 0
                #audio.rate = 192000
            }
        }
    }
]
```

@serhii-nakon Thank you for your efforts investigating the problem of the mic input low gain problem. My experience with the ALC4080 chipset in my ASUS "ROG STRIX B650E-I GAMING WIFI" motherboard is a bit of a mixed bag; the analog output is pretty good, can go up to crazy sampling rates (like 32 bit/384 kHz). One could argue it is too loud (beware to not blow off your ears if using a headset!). I'll try to set a volume cap on it via a new feature in pipewire (https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/pipewire/pipewire/-/commit/fb74ab9054cb625aeff4e271e9134b0fd0cdcfde). The mic input, as noted by others, appears to lack amplification and is thus currently useless (I'm using my webcam microphone in the meantime). I haven't tried the "line in" input; I suppose it suffers from the same tiny gain problem as the mic input?

Another observation: I'm now using the S/PDIF (Toslink) digital output to drive a hi-fi amplifier that is specified to accept 24 bit / 192 kHz digital S/PDIF via a high quality cable rated for 192 kHz. When configured in pipewire to use 192 kHz, the sound tends to crackle/pops subtly; I haven't been able to pinpoint whether this is caused by the ALC4080 S/PDIF output or the amplifier input; given the amplifier is from a serious brand (Totem Acoustic), I suspect the ALC4080. I've reached out to ASUS Support to know if this was perhaps a known issue, but haven't heard back from them yet.

EDIT: ASUS Support took more than a week to tell me to contact the local distributor for their products here in Japan. I doubt they'll know anything about known issues about using 192 kHz rate over the S/PDIF output, but I'll reaching them out anyway.
